WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.920
There are some strong contenders like:

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.620
"The Judgement of Cambyses" by Gerard David,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.320
Hieronymus Bosch's rendering of Hell,

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.420
Henry Fuseli's "Nightmare",

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.060
and Edvard Munch's "The Scream".

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.720
But there's only ever really been one painting that has seriously disturbed me --

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:46.920
this one.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.000
Francisco Goya's "Saturn Devouring His Son".

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.480
What you're seeing here,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.960
is the legendary Spanish painter's depiction of the Greek Titan Cronus,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.880
who after usurping power from his father,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.020
was told a prophecy that one of his own sons would do the same and usurp him.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:10.940
In order to prevent this, every time his Queen Rhea bore a child, Cronus would eat it.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.880
Unfortunately for him, in the end Rhea conspired to hide away their youngest son --

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:16.860
Zeus;

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.120
who eventually fulfilled the prophecy, exiled his father and ended the reign of the Titans.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.460
The story is a well known Greek myth, but look at how Goya handles it.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.980
Some key changes jump out right away.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.380
First, in the myth Cronus devours his children by swallowing them whole.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.560
In fact, they remain alive in his stomach.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.420
Goya's painting is a much more gruesome affair.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.860
He takes some inspiration here from Peter Paul Rubens,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.060
a Flemish baroque painter who depicted the same event as well.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.880
In Rubens' "Saturn", the Titan seems to be sucking the life force from his child.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.540
Even for such a terrifying subject matter,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.480
Rubens displays all the drama, richness, even beauty,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.820
that marks the Baroque style he helped to make famous.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.160
In Goya's version, that beauty is gone.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.600
We're left with a frightened, crazed monster

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.860
discovered in the dark as if by some explorer with a torch,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.680
who wandered into the wrong cave.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.040
Saturn -- Cronus' Roman name --

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.120
has already chewed off the head of his child.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.940
His black mouth opens around the elbow of the left arm,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.040
ready to bite it off at the joint.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.620
His angular body is crouching in an awkward position,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.160
his hands dig into the spine,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.120
blood runs down his child's arm

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.500
and neck and shoulder a startling primary color.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.260
And if we take a closer look,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.340
we noticed that this is not a child at all --

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.840
but one of Saturn's kids grown up.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.460
There's something even more terrifying

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.040
in knowing that the victim knew what was happening and tried to fight back.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.960
But what's most disturbing of all, I think,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.420
is when and where this painting was found.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.080
Late in his life, Francisco Goya purchased a house on the outskirts of Madrid,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.000
called "La Quinta del Sordo",

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.180
or "The Villa of the Deaf" after its previous owner.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.020
An interesting coincidence, since by that time in his life,

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.520
Goya was deaf too.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.820
His physical and mental health declining,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.940
Goya painted 14 murals often referred to as

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.100
"The Black Paintings".

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.300
Directly onto the interior walls of his home.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.880
"Saturn devouring his son"?

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.520
Was in the dining room.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.140
The photographs you're seeing now were taken over 50 years after that time.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.180
Goya never mentioned the paintings himself.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.340
He never intended for anyone to see them

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.700
but to this day, people still puzzle over the meaning of "The Black Paintings".

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:46.820
Why was Goya creating these pessimistic and fantastical scenes in the solitude of his home?

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.960
To understand this it might help to go back through Goya's career.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.520
He grew up in Zaragoza, Spain. The fourth of six children in a lower middle-class family.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:03.700
By all accounts, he was a light-hearted and joyful kid as he studied painting in Zaragoza, Madrid and Rome.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:11.900
His first serious job was at the royal tapestry factory where he created tapestry cartoons to adorn the palaces and stately homes of the city.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.900
These tapestries take their cue from

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.780
"The Rococo Style",

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:22.850
elegant, playful, light scenes of both nobility and peasantry enjoying the normal activities of their day.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.100
Goya eventually became the court painter for King Charles IV, a disappointing monarch

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:35.360
unlike his father Charle III, who was beloved by the people for enacting reforms that began to bring

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.359
secular enlightenment values to Spain.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.880
In 1793, an unknown illness

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.650
left Goya deaf.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:49.720
Though he still took commissions from his royal clientele, this disease was a dark turning point in his life and art.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:53.000
You can see it in "Yard With Lunatics" from 1794

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.480
bodies grapple and cry out in anguish.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.340
The difference between this and the tapestry cartoons,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.100
is shocking.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:08.500
His hearing gone, Goya began to see the country around him with a grim clarity in a series of etchings called

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:15.100
Los Caprichos or The Caprices, he sends up a Spanish culture that is both tragic and comic.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.060
A student of the Enlightenment himself, Goya sees the country

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.340
backsliding on the road to modernity. The King is withdrawn and the people are

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.900
superstitious and too stupid to know what they need.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:31.840
You can see him fusing all the corners of his imagination the quest for anatomical truth,

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.980
the need for social critique and an obsession with beasts and creatures.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.960
It's all summed up in this one aptly titled "The Sleep of Reason produces monsters".

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.000
In the following years, things got worse for Spain.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.180
Napoleon invaded the country and brutally massacred those who resisted his campaign.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.080
Goya was witness to the bloodshed and it affected him deeply.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.300
His painting on the subject, the 3rd of May 1808, is a revelatory depiction of

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.240
war, resistance and brutality.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.460
Before this, war was a highly composed theatrical subject in painting.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.040
Here, Goya gives nothing but brute force;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.220
it's emotion unmediated by artifice.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.560
It would be five years until Spain regained its throne.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.340
In the interim resistors developed The Constitution of 1812,

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.800
which called for liberal reforms like: national sovereignty,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.380
freedom of the press and free enterprise.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.980
But on gaining power, the new King Ferdinand VII

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.860
squashed the Constitution right away,

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.520
and arrested those who made it.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.220
Goya, withdrew, disheartened

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:46.940
the country which in his youth had reached toward a new world, was now swallowed again by autocracy.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:54.000
Scarred by war, scarred by illness, he began to paint nightmare scenes onto the walls of his home.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:07:01.100
In one, a young man is being eaten by the father he was prophesied to usurp.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:07.280
By now Goya knows progress isn't assured and when it's defeated, it's not painless

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.840
It's horrific and slow and the victim can feel it happening.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.900
There are a lot of ways that you can read "Saturn devouring his son".

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:22.100
Maybe Goya was trying to exorcise the demons of his mind or the demons of his country.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:27.380
Or maybe he was just trying to paint honestly about one terrifying facet of man's nature,

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:32.580
using the skills and techniques he learned and pioneered through a lifetime.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.520
"The Black Paintings" changed the history of art, but what's maybe scariest of all

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.160
is that Goya didn't care.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:46.900
He doesn't care how we read this painting because he didn't paint it for us or for anyone.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.020
"Saturn devouring his son" exists beyond interpretation.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.580
It's brute force horror without mediation.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:01.500
A monster looking out from a dark wall in a dark room,

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.280
...chewing...

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.300
Hey everybody, thank you so much for watching.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.580
This episode was brought to you by Squarespace

